Q: Can I use StreamingResponseBody with a generic ResponseEntity&lt;?&gt; A: <p>This example works as you want.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@RestController
public class WebDavController {
    @RequestMapping(&quot;/{filename}&quot;)
    private ResponseEntity&lt;String&gt; handlePropFind(HttpServletRequest request) {
        if (request.getMethod().equals(&quot;PROPFIND&quot;)) {
            return ResponseEntity.ok(&quot;this is a test&quot;);
        }
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.METHOD_NOT_ALLOWED).build();
    }

    @GetMapping(&quot;/{filename}&quot;)
    private ResponseEntity&lt;StreamingResponseBody&gt; handleGet(@PathVariable String filename) {
        StreamingResponseBody responseBody =
                outputStream -&gt; Files.copy(Path.of(filename), outputStream);
        return ResponseEntity.ok()
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_OCTET_STREAM)
                .body(responseBody);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>@RequestMapping</code> without a method definition works for all HTTP methods, but doesn't override method-specific mappings like <code>@GetMapping</code>.</p>

Q: Stupid if else discussion i had A: <p>Consider you have not 3 cases but 5:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>if (a) {
  code = a;
} else {
  if (b) {
    code = b;
  } else {
    if (c) {
      code = c;
    } else {
      if (d) {
        code = d;
      } else {
        code = e;
      }
    }
  } 
}
</code></pre>
<p>Don't you agree that the following reads a whole lot better?</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>if (a) {
  code = a;
} else if (b) {
  code = b;
} else if (c) {
  code = c;
} else if (d) {
  code = d;
} else {
  code = e;
}
</code></pre>
<p>You are right: each <code>if</code> statement in each <code>else</code> statement is a single statement, and usually single statements should be enclosed in curly braces. But for <code>else if</code> the general consensus is that you can make an exception for it. Other languages even have different keywords for this: <code>elif</code> in Python and <code>elseif</code> in PHP effectively. PHP does allow <code>else if</code> but my static analysis tools give a warning about it.</p>
